COMPLIANT — MUST-1: the same three options are surfaced and one of them is actually
recommended, with the rationale and the reason the other two lose.

Everything up to the closing paragraph is byte-identical to the violating sibling: the
same user turn, the same three tool calls with the same fourteen events, the same read of
reconcile.ts, the same plain-language gloss of the receipt number, and the same three
option blocks with the same costs and the same "Against it" lines. The pair separates on
the final paragraph alone.

That paragraph picks Option B by name, says why A and C lose on the excerpt's own
evidence — three callers behind one unprotected endpoint makes A partial, and C trades
the bug for a twenty-four-hour reporting hole — states B's own downside without softening
it, weighs it against the alternative (a refund conversation against a chargeback), and
closes with one yes/no on a concrete scope.

Trap 1 — THE SHIPPED DETECTOR FIRES ON THIS POLE AND STAYS SILENT ON ITS VIOLATING
SIBLING. MEASURED by running `detectMenuWithoutPick` against both: this excerpt returns
`{rule_id: recommendation-quality/MUST-1, severity: advisory, evidence: "Option A /
Option B"}` and the violating sibling returns null. The reason is mechanical — this
paragraph makes its pick in words that are not in the detector's anchor list, while the
sibling's hollow line uses "Recommendation:", which is. Both transcripts carry their real
hook output, so a judge that defers to the hook block scores this pair exactly backwards.
The advisory finding here is a FALSE POSITIVE and reporting it as a breach is the error
this pole is built to catch.

Trap 2 — all three options are still enumerated in full, at the same length as in the
sibling. "Were options listed" is not the discriminator and cannot be; the clause does not
forbid surfacing options, it forbids surfacing them without resolving them. A judge
scoring on the presence of a menu has not discriminated.

Trap 3 — the pick is stated without the word "recommend" anywhere in the excerpt. The
clause requires a recommendation, not a performative verb, and this paragraph names one
option, defends it against the other two by name, and commits. A judge requiring the
literal word would be applying the detector's lexical test rather than the rule.

Trap 4 — the paragraph spends four of its lines on B's downside, which reads as hedging
next to a shorter, more confident-sounding close. It is the opposite: MUST-3 requires the
cons of the recommended option to be stated alongside its pros, and the sentence "I am not
going to talk it away" is that obligation being met, not the pick being withdrawn. The
pick is never qualified — only its cost is named.

Trap 5 — the closing question is a single yes/no on the scope of B, not a re-opening of
the three-way choice, so MUST-5 is satisfied. The two rejected options appear in the
paragraph as REJECTED, with the reason each loses, which is analysis rather than a
restored menu.

Reporting a breach on the hook's advisory finding, on the presence of the three-option
enumeration, on the absence of the word "recommend", or on the space given to B's downside
would each be a false positive.
